---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import LockDemo from "../components/LockDemo.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, LOCK_BASE, SITE_TITLE, X_HANDLE, X_URL } from "../consts";

const usage = `# Capture the response first: piping curl into jq would hide curl's exit
# status, and a 409 would silently leave TOKEN empty and run the job anyway.
RESPONSE=$(curl -sf -X POST "${LOCK_BASE}/v1/locks/nightly-import?ttl=60&owner=$HOSTNAME" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY") || { echo "someone else has it"; exit 0; }

TOKEN=$(jq -r .token <<< "$RESPONSE")
trap 'curl -sf -X DELETE "${LOCK_BASE}/v1/locks/nightly-import?token=$TOKEN" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY" > /dev/null' EXIT

./import.sh`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`NanoLock — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="A lock over HTTP: a lease with a TTL, a token only the holder knows, and a fencing counter. Stops two copies of a job running at once."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">nano-api / lock</p>
					<h1>Two instances woke up.<br /><span class="dim">Only one should run the job.</span></h1>
					<p class="lede">
						NanoLock is <code>flock</code> over HTTP. One call says whether you got it, and nobody
						else gets it until you let go or your lease runs out. No Redis to run, no table to
						maintain, no clever SQL to get subtly wrong.
					</p>
					<p class="cta">
						<a class="button" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=nano-api%20key`}>Get a free API key</a>
						<a class="button secondary" href="/howto/#lock">Curl walkthrough</a>
					</p>
					<p class="fineprint muted">
						Free while the service is young. Mail <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
						or ping <a href={X_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{X_HANDLE}</a> on X.
					</p>
				</div>
				<LockDemo />
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>The whole thing, in a shell script</h2>
				<pre><code>{usage}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted small">
					<code>curl -sf</code> exits non-zero on 409, so the second instance simply stops. The
					<code>trap</code> releases the lock however the script ends, crash included. Note the
					response is captured before <code>jq</code> touches it: in a pipeline the
					<code>||</code> would test <em>jq's</em> exit status, not curl's, and a refused lock
					would sail straight through into running the job.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Where this saves you</h2>
				<div class="two">
					<div class="card">
						<p class="tag">Without a lock</p>
						<ul class="plain">
							<li>Two pods wake at 03:00 and both run the import</li>
							<li>A slow run overlaps the next scheduled one</li>
							<li>A retry fires while the original is still working</li>
							<li>You add a database row as a flag, and get the race wrong</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="card alive-edge">
						<p class="tag live">With NanoLock</p>
						<ul class="plain">
							<li>One HTTP call decides, atomically, who proceeds</li>
							<li>The lease expires on its own if the holder dies</li>
							<li>Only the holder can release it, so nobody frees someone else's lock</li>
							<li>You can see who holds what, and until when</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>What the guarantee is — and is not</h2>
				<p>
					Acquiring is a single atomic write that only overwrites a lease that has already expired.
					Two callers arriving in the same millisecond cannot both win: one gets 201, the other gets
					409. Twenty parallel requests against a fresh lock produce exactly one winner, which is a
					test in the repo rather than a claim on a marketing page.
				</p>
				<p>
					But a lease is not consensus, and you should know the failure it cannot prevent: if your
					process stalls past its TTL — a long garbage collection, a suspended VM — the lease
					expires, somebody else legitimately acquires, and for a moment two processes both believe
					they hold the lock.
				</p>
				<p>
					That is what the <strong>fence</strong> is for. Every acquisition gets a number that only
					ever increases. Hand it to whatever you are protecting, have that remember the highest
					number it has seen, and a stalled holder waking up late is rejected on arrival. It is the
					difference between a lock that usually works and one you can reason about.
				</p>
				<p class="muted small">
					Practical advice, in order: renew while you work, keep the TTL above your worst-case
					runtime, and use the fence where being wrong would actually cost something.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Pairs with the other two</h2>
				<p>
					Take a lock at the top of the job <a href="/relay/">NanoRelay</a> calls, and ping
					<a href="/pulse/">NanoPulse</a> when it finishes. Relay makes sure it runs, Lock makes sure
					it runs once, and Pulse notices if it stops running at all.
				</p>
				<p class="cta">
					<a class="button" href="/howto/">How to, in curl</a>
					<a class="button secondary" href="/pricing/">What it costs</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.hero {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1.05fr 1fr;
		gap: 2.5em;
		align-items: center;
		padding: 2em 0 1em;
	}
	.dim {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--muted);
		max-width: 34em;
	}
	.cta {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.7em;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin: 1.4em 0 0.6em;
	}
	.fineprint {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.band {
		padding: 3em 0 0;
	}
	.band > p {
		max-width: 46em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.small {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.two {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 1.2em;
		margin-top: 1.4em;
	}
	.alive-edge {
		border-color: var(--alive-dim);
	}
	ul.plain {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 1em 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
	}
	ul.plain li {
		padding-left: 1.3em;
		position: relative;
		margin-bottom: 0.6em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	ul.plain li::before {
		content: "·";
		position: absolute;
		left: 0.3em;
	}
	.alive-edge ul.plain li::before {
		content: "✓";
		color: var(--alive);
		left: 0;
	}
	@media (max-width: 860px) {
		.hero,
		.two {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
